iability, stress resilience, weather assessment, technical. Concerns: memory for numbers (attention/accuracy), nasty remarks (crew cooperation, professional communication). That is serious.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, but difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education. Strengths: decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence. Concerns: communication, teamwork, adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, but regarded as know-it-all, hot temper. Strengths: adaptability, attention, problem solving, responsibility. Concerns: know-it-all (cooperation), hot temper (stress resilience, crew cooperation). \n\nWe need best overall fit. Candidate A has strong technical and leadership but issues with openness. Candidate B has reliability and stress but communication issues. Candidate C strong decision and technical but egocentric and communication. Candidate D strong problem solving and responsibility but hot temper.\n\nWhich is best? Probably Candidate A maybe best technical and leadership, but show-off and not open to new ideas could affect crew. Candidate B's nasty remarks are serious for crew cooperation. Candidate C's egocentric and unwillingness to learn is also concerning. Candidate D's hot temper is concerning for stress and crew. So maybe Candidate A is best despite show-off? Show-off may be less severe than nasty remarks or hot temper. Not open to new ideas could affect adaptability but maybe manageable.\n\nThus I will recommend Candidate A, stating reasons and acknowledging concerns.\n\nWe need to reference existing discussion - none, but we can say \"Given the facts we have so far\". That's okay.\n\nProvide metadata vote: Candidate A.\n\nProceed."}
